Thought: To identify the SMILES string of the organic sample, I should start by gathering some initial data about the molecule. The mass spectrometry spectra can provide the molecular weight and potential molecular formula, which is a good starting point for identifying the compound. This will help narrow down the possibilities and guide further analysis.
Action: mass_spectrometry_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The mass spectrometry data indicates a molecular ion peak at m/z 100.05, suggesting a molecular weight of approximately 100 g/mol. This information can be used to deduce the molecular formula. Next, I will use the carbon NMR spectra to gather more information about the structure of the molecule, which will help in identifying the SMILES string.
Action: carbon_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The carbon NMR spectra show chemical shifts at 135.80, 117.40, 70.50, 54.00, and 43.70 ppm. These shifts suggest the presence of different types of carbon environments, possibly including aromatic carbons (135.80, 117.40 ppm), an alcohol or ether (70.50 ppm), and aliphatic carbons (54.00, 43.70 ppm). To further narrow down the structure, I will now check the proton NMR spectra to identify the hydrogen environments, which will complement the carbon NMR data.
Action: proton_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}
